==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*selh₁-}}
From {{bor|en|frm|consulte}}. In sense “council”, it represents {{bor|en|la|cōnsultum}}, {{bor|en|it|consulto}}; and it may have been often taken as a direct formation from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''<sup>1</sup>|noformat=1|id=1095693013}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED2|pos=''n.''<sup>1</sup>|noformat=1|id=00048246}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|kŏn'sŭlt ''or'' kənsŭlt'}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒnsʌlt/|/kənˈsʌlt/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑnsʌlt/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-consult-noun.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌlt|s=2}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|now|US|countable}} A visit to consult somebody, such as a doctor; a [[consultation]].
#: {{syn|en|consultation}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The act of consulting or deliberating; consultation.
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|chapter=Of the Iewes|page=201|passage=For firſt upon '''conſult''' of reaſon, there will bee found no eaſie aſſurance for to faſten a materiall or temperamentall propriety upon any nation; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The result of consultation; determination; decision.
#* {{RQ:Homer Dryden Iliad|title=Ilias 1|page=431|passage=[T]he council broke; / And all their grave '''conſults''' diſſolv'd in ſmoke.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A council; a meeting for consultation.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1730|author=w:Jonathan Swift|title=Death and Daphne|chapter=5
|passage=a '''consult''' of coquettes}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Agreement; concert.

=====Usage notes=====
* {{sense|visit to consult somebody}} The noun {{m|en|consult}} is avoided in British English, where {{m|en|consultation}} is preferred. In American English, they are merely synonyms.

===Etymology 2===
From {{bor|en|frm|consulter}}, from {{der|en|la|cōnsultō||to deliberate, consult}}, frequentative of {{m|la|cōnsulō||to consult, deliberate, consider, reflect upon, ask advice}}, from {{m|la|com-||together}} + {{m|la||-sulō}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*selh₁-|t=to take, grab}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|kənsŭlt'}}, {{IPA|en|/kənˈsʌlt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-consult-verb.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌlt|s=2}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To seek the opinion or advice of another; to take counsel; to deliberate together; to confer.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 3|act=V|scene=iii|passage=Let us '''consult''' upon to-morrow's business.}}
#* '''1661''' (written), published in '''1681''', {{w|Thomas Hobbes}}, ''A Dialogue between a Philosopher and a Student of the Common Laws of England''
#*: All the laws of England have been made by the kings of England, '''consulting''' with the nobility and commons.
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=January 11, 1889
|origdate=December 21, 1888
|author=Kung Taotai
|translator=M. F. A. Fraser
|title=North Honan Road.
|journal=North-China Herald and Supreme Court & Consular Gazette
|url=https://archive.org/details/north-china-herald-1889.01.10/
|location=Shanghai
|volume=XLII
|issue=1119
|oclc=311405954
|page=37
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/north-china-herald-1889.01.10/page/37/
|column=1
|text=S<small>IR</small>,—I have the honour to refer to your letter requesting me to desire Mr. Y. Ching-chong to come and '''consult''' with the Municipal Council, as Mr. Wood, the Chairman of that Body had informed you at a personal interview that they fully concurred in the desirability of co-operation with Mr. Ching-chong in improvements in the Honan road draining and lighting.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[advise]] or offer expertise.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To work as a [[consultant]] or [[contractor]] rather than as a full-time [[employee]] of a firm.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To ask advice of; to seek the opinion of (a person)
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1899|author=w:John Cotton Dana|title=A Library Primer| chapter=1| passage=If you have no library commission, '''consult''' a lawyer and get from him a careful statement of what can be done under present statutory regulations.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[refer]] to (something) for information.
#: {{coordinate terms|en|look up}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1904|author=w:Guy Wetmore Carryl|title=Far from the Maddening Girls| chapter=3| passage=Which reminds me that I have never remembered from that hour to '''consult''' the dictionary upon a selvage.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1837|author=w:William Whewell|title=History of the Inductive Sciences
|passage=Men forgot, or feared, to '''consult''' nature, to seek for new truths, to do what the great discoverers of other times had done; they were content to '''consult''' libraries.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To have reference to, in judging or acting; to have regard to; to consider; as, to consult one's wishes.
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|passage=We are {{...}} to '''consult''' the necessities of life, rather than matters of ornament and delight.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To deliberate upon; to take for.
#* {{RQ:Clarendon History|passage=Many things were there '''consulted''' for the future, yet nothing was positively resolved.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To bring about by counsel or contrivance; to devise; to contrive.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Habakkuk|2|10|passage=Thou hast '''consulted''' shame to thy house by cutting off many people.}}

